[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Core Java: Data Types, Variables, Operators, and Control Flow"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"This tutorial covers fundamental concepts in Core Java: data types, variables, operators, and control flow statements. Understanding these concepts is crucial for building any Java application."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"Data Types"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Java is a statically-typed language, meaning that every variable must be declared with a specific data type. Java provides two categories of data types: primitive and non-primitive (reference)."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Primitive Data Types"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"Primitive data types are the basic building blocks of data manipulation. They are predefined by the language and named by a keyword. Java has eight primitive data types:"},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`byte`: 8-bit signed integer. Range: -128 to 127. Default value: 0."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`short`: 16-bit signed integer. Range: -32,768 to 32,767. Default value: 0."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`int`: 32-bit signed integer. Range: -2,147,483,648 to 2,147,483,647. Default value: 0."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`long`: 64-bit signed integer. Range: -9,223,372,036,854,775,808 to 9,223,372,036,854,775,807. Default value: 0L."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`float`: 32-bit single-precision floating-point number. Follows IEEE 754 standard. Default value: 0.0f."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`double`: 64-bit double-precision floating-point number. Follows IEEE 754 standard. Default value: 0.0d."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`char`: 16-bit Unicode character. Represents a single character. Default value: '\u0000' (null character)."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`boolean`: Represents a logical value. Can be either `true` or `false`. Default value: `false`."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"public class PrimitiveDataTypes {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        byte myByte = 100;
        short myShort = 10000;
        int myInt = 100000;
        long myLong = 1000000000L;

        float myFloat = 3.14f;
        double myDouble = 3.14159;

        char myChar = 'A';
        boolean myBoolean = true;

        System.out.println("Byte: " + myByte);
        System.out.println("Short: " + myShort);
        System.out.println("Int: " + myInt);
        System.out.println("Long: " + myLong);
        System.out.println("Float: " + myFloat);
        System.out.println("Double: " + myDouble);
        System.out.println("Char: " + myChar);
        System.out.println("Boolean: " + myBoolean);
    }
}"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Non-Primitive (Reference) Data Types"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"Non-primitive data types are created by the programmer and are not predefined by Java. They are also called reference types because they store the memory address of the object they refer to. Examples include:"},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`String`: Represents a sequence of characters."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`Arrays`: Represents a collection of elements of the same data type."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`Classes`: Blueprint for creating objects."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`Interfaces`: A contract that a class can implement."},{"order":22,"type":"code","text":"public class ReferenceDataTypes {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String myString = "Hello, World!";
        int[] myArray = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};

        System.out.println("String: " + myString);
        System.out.print("Array: ");
        for (int i = 0; i < myArray.length; i++) {
            System.out.print(myArray[i] + " ");
        }
        System.out.println();
    }
}"},{"order":23,"type":"heading","text":"Variables"},{"order":24,"type":"paragraph","text":"A variable is a named storage location in the computer's memory that can hold a value. In Java, you must declare a variable before you can use it. The declaration specifies the variable's data type and name."},{"order":25,"type":"subheading","text":"Variable Declaration and Initialization"},{"order":26,"type":"paragraph","text":"To declare a variable, use the following syntax:"},{"order":27,"type":"code","text":"dataType variableName;"},{"order":28,"type":"paragraph","text":"To initialize a variable, assign a value to it using the assignment operator (=):"},{"order":29,"type":"code","text":"variableName = value;"},{"order":30,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can also declare and initialize a variable in a single statement:"},{"order":31,"type":"code","text":"dataType variableName = value;"},{"order":32,"type":"code","text":"public class VariableExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int age;
        age = 30;

        String name = "John Doe";

        System.out.println("Name: " + name);
        System.out.println("Age: " + age);
    }
}"},{"order":33,"type":"subheading","text":"Variable Scope"},{"order":34,"type":"paragraph","text":"The scope of a variable determines where in the code it can be accessed. Java has three types of variable scope:"},{"order":35,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Local variables: Declared inside a method or block of code. They are only accessible within that method or block."},{"order":36,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Instance variables: Declared inside a class but outside any method. They belong to an instance of the class (an object) and are accessible from any method within the class."},{"order":37,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Static variables: Declared inside a class but outside any method, using the `static` keyword. They belong to the class itself and are shared by all instances of the class."},{"order":38,"type":"code","text":"public class ScopeExample {
    static int staticVariable = 100; // Static variable
    int instanceVariable = 50; // Instance variable

    public void myMethod() {
        int localVariable = 25; // Local variable
        System.out.println("Local variable: " + localVariable);
        System.out.println("Instance variable: " + instanceVariable);
        System.out.println("Static variable: " + staticVariable);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ScopeExample obj = new ScopeExample();
        obj.myMethod();

        System.out.println("Static variable (from main): " + ScopeExample.staticVariable);
    }
}"},{"order":39,"type":"heading","text":"Operators"},{"order":40,"type":"paragraph","text":"Operators are special symbols that perform specific operations on one, two, or three operands. Java provides a rich set of operators, including:"},{"order":41,"type":"subheading","text":"Arithmetic Operators"},{"order":42,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`+` (Addition)"},{"order":43,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`-` (Subtraction)"},{"order":44,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`*` (Multiplication)"},{"order":45,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`/` (Division)"},{"order":46,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`%` (Modulo - remainder of division)"},{"order":47,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`++` (Increment)"},{"order":48,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`--` (Decrement)"},{"order":49,"type":"code","text":"public class ArithmeticOperators {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int a = 10;
        int b = 5;

        System.out.println("a + b = " + (a + b));
        System.out.println("a - b = " + (a - b));
        System.out.println("a * b = " + (a * b));
        System.out.println("a / b = " + (a / b));
        System.out.println("a % b = " + (a % b));

        a++;
        System.out.println("a++ = " + a);

        b--;
        System.out.println("b-- = " + b);
    }
}"},{"order":50,"type":"subheading","text":"Assignment Operators"},{"order":51,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`=` (Assignment)"},{"order":52,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`+=` (Add and assign)"},{"order":53,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`−=` (Subtract and assign)"},{"order":54,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`*=` (Multiply and assign)"},{"order":55,"type":"bullet_point","text":"` /=` (Divide and assign)"},{"order":56,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`%=` (Modulo and assign)"},{"order":57,"type":"code","text":"public class AssignmentOperators {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int a = 10;

        a += 5; // a = a + 5
        System.out.println("a += 5: " + a);

        a -= 3; // a = a - 3
        System.out.println("a -= 3: " + a);

        a *= 2; // a = a * 2
        System.out.println("a *= 2: " + a);

        a /= 4; // a = a / 4
        System.out.println("a /= 4: " + a);

        a %= 3; // a = a % 3
        System.out.println("a %= 3: " + a);
    }
}"},{"order":58,"type":"subheading","text":"Comparison Operators"},{"order":59,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`==` (Equal to)"},{"order":60,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`!=` (Not equal to)"},{"order":61,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`>` (Greater than)"},{"order":62,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`<` (Less than)"},{"order":63,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`>=` (Greater than or equal to)"},{"order":64,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`<=` (Less than or equal to)"},{"order":65,"type":"code","text":"public class ComparisonOperators {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int a = 10;
        int b = 5;

        System.out.println("a == b: " + (a == b));
        System.out.println("a != b: " + (a != b));
        System.out.println("a > b: " + (a > b));
        System.out.println("a < b: " + (a < b));
        System.out.println("a >= b: " + (a >= b));
        System.out.println("a <= b: " + (a <= b));
    }
}"},{"order":66,"type":"subheading","text":"Logical Operators"},{"order":67,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`&&` (Logical AND)"},{"order":68,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`||` (Logical OR)"},{"order":69,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`!` (Logical NOT)"},{"order":70,"type":"code","text":"public class LogicalOperators {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        boolean x = true;
        boolean y = false;

        System.out.println("x && y: " + (x && y));
        System.out.println("x || y: " + (x || y));
        System.out.println("!x: " + (!x));
    }
}"},{"order":71,"type":"heading","text":"Control Flow"},{"order":72,"type":"paragraph","text":"Control flow statements determine the order in which statements are executed in a program. Java provides several control flow statements:"},{"order":73,"type":"subheading","text":"Conditional Statements"},{"order":74,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`if` statement: Executes a block of code if a condition is true."},{"order":75,"type":"code","text":"if (condition) {
    // Code to execute if the condition is true
}"},{"order":76,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`if-else` statement: Executes one block of code if a condition is true and another block of code if the condition is false."},{"order":77,"type":"code","text":"if (condition) {
    // Code to execute if the condition is true
} else {
    // Code to execute if the condition is false
}"},{"order":78,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`if-else if-else` statement: Executes different blocks of code based on multiple conditions."},{"order":79,"type":"code","text":"if (condition1) {
    // Code to execute if condition1 is true
} else if (condition2) {
    // Code to execute if condition2 is true
} else {
    // Code to execute if none of the conditions are true
}"},{"order":80,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`switch` statement: Executes different blocks of code based on the value of an expression."},{"order":81,"type":"code","text":"switch (expression) {
    case value1:
        // Code to execute if expression == value1
        break;
    case value2:
        // Code to execute if expression == value2
        break;
    default:
        // Code to execute if expression doesn't match any value
}"},{"order":82,"type":"code","text":"public class ConditionalStatements {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int age = 20;

        if (age >= 18) {
            System.out.println("You are eligible to vote.");
        } else {
            System.out.println("You are not eligible to vote.");
        }

        int day = 3;
        switch (day) {
            case 1:
                System.out.println("Monday");
                break;
            case 2:
                System.out.println("Tuesday");
                break;
            case 3:
                System.out.println("Wednesday");
                break;
            default:
                System.out.println("Invalid day");
        }
    }
}"},{"order":83,"type":"subheading","text":"Looping Statements"},{"order":84,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`for` loop: Executes a block of code repeatedly for a specified number of times."},{"order":85,"type":"code","text":"for (initialization; condition; increment/decrement) {
    // Code to execute repeatedly
}"},{"order":86,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`while` loop: Executes a block of code repeatedly as long as a condition is true."},{"order":87,"type":"code","text":"while (condition) {
    // Code to execute repeatedly
}"},{"order":88,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`do-while` loop: Executes a block of code at least once, and then repeatedly as long as a condition is true."},{"order":89,"type":"code","text":"do {
    // Code to execute repeatedly
} while (condition);"},{"order":90,"type":"code","text":"public class LoopingStatements {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // For loop
        for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
            System.out.println("For loop: " + i);
        }

        // While loop
        int j = 0;
        while (j < 5) {
            System.out.println("While loop: " + j);
            j++;
        }

        // Do-while loop
        int k = 0;
        do {
            System.out.println("Do-while loop: " + k);
            k++;
        } while (k < 5);
    }
}"},{"order":91,"type":"subheading","text":"Jump Statements"},{"order":92,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`break` statement: Terminates the execution of a loop or switch statement."},{"order":93,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`continue` statement: Skips the current iteration of a loop and proceeds to the next iteration."},{"order":94,"type":"code","text":"public class JumpStatements {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Break statement
        for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
            if (i == 5) {
                break; // Exit the loop when i is 5
            }
            System.out.println("Break: " + i);
        }

        // Continue statement
        for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
            if (i % 2 == 0) {
                continue; // Skip even numbers
            }
            System.out.println("Continue: " + i);
        }
    }
}"},{"order":95,"type":"heading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":96,"type":"paragraph","text":"This tutorial covered the fundamental concepts of data types, variables, operators, and control flow in Java. Mastering these concepts is essential for writing effective and efficient Java programs. Practice these concepts with various examples to solidify your understanding."}]
